top_10.body:
A fragment entry parameter bound to `gl_HelperInvocation` has no Slang system value, but Slang can read it with `IsHelperLane()`. HLSL output already turns such a parameter into a local read from the intrinsic, and Slang output does the same for `gl_NumWorkGroups` with `WorkgroupCount()` (`SlangCodeGen.slang_num_workgroups_parameter_alias`).

```glsl
shader Probe {
    fragment {
        vec4 main(vec2 uv @ TEXCOORD0, bool helper @ gl_HelperInvocation) @ gl_FragColor {
            return vec4(uv, helper ? 1.0 : 0.0, 1.0);
        }
    }
}
```

```bash
crosstl translate helper_param.cgl --backend slang --output helper_param.slang
slangc helper_param.slang -target spirv-asm -entry main -stage fragment -o -
```

On main at e78faffb, the Slang generator writes the semantic unchanged:

```slang
[shader("fragment")] float4 main(float2 uv : TEXCOORD0, bool helper : gl_HelperInvocation) : SV_Target
```

slangc 2026.9.1 accepts this as a user semantic and declares `helper` as an ordinary input varying (`OpDecorate %helper Location 1`). No stage writes it, and the shader never reads the helper-invocation state. After #3649, the Slang generator refuses the parameter with `SlangLoweringRefused: Slang has no system value for the built-in @gl_HelperInvocation` instead.

The HLSL generator writes `float4 PSMain(float2 uv : TEXCOORD0) : SV_TARGET { bool helper = IsHelperLane(); ... }` for the same source.

Expected: in a fragment entry point, Slang output drops the parameter from the signature and declares `bool helper = IsHelperLane();` at the start of the body, as for `gl_NumWorkGroups`. In other stages it refuses the parameter with `SlangLoweringRefused`. The struct-member form (`bool helper @ gl_HelperInvocation;` in the stage input) is the lowering #3541 asks for HLSL and Metal.

Found while reviewing #3649.

```cuda
extern "C" __global__ void first(float* out) {
    static __constant__ float local_table[4];
    unsigned int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    out[i] = local_table[i % 4];
}
```

clang accepts it (`xcrun clang++ -x cuda --cuda-device-only --cuda-gpu-arch=sm_70 -nocudainc -nocudalib -fsyntax-only`, and clang 23 with `-x hip --cuda-device-only --offload-arch=gfx90a -nogpuinc -nogpulib -fsyntax-only` for the same source as `.hip`). A `static` local has static storage, so without an initializer every element is zero, and device code cannot write `__constant__` memory, so the kernel writes zeros.

CUDA and HIP input on main at 2c01bef1 put a resource declaration inside the function body, at the binding of the kernel's first buffer:

```
fn first(
    @group(0) @binding(0) var<storage, read_write> out: array<f32>
) {
    @group(0) @binding(0) var<uniform> local_table: array<f32, 4>;
    var i: u32 = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    out[i] = local_table[(i % 4)];
}
```

Metal output declares an uninitialized local array, which `xcrun -sdk macosx metal -c` compiles, so the kernel reads indeterminate values instead of zeros:

```metal
kernel void kernel_first(uint3 thread_position_in_grid
                         [[thread_position_in_grid]],
                         device float *out [[buffer(0)]]) {
  float local_table[4];
  uint i = thread_position_in_grid.x;
  out[i] = local_table[i % 4];
}
```

HIP input gives the same Metal body. With an initializer, `static __constant__ float local_table[4] = {1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f};`, Metal output keeps the values (`float local_table[4] = {1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0};`).

Expected: CUDA and HIP input import a `static __constant__` local without an initializer as zero-initialized constant data (for example a function-scope constant array of zeros), or refuse it with a typed diagnostic; it is never read uninitialized, and it does not take a resource binding.

Found while reviewing #3650.

```c
__constant float coeffs_table[4] = {1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f};

__kernel void first(__global float* out) {
    size_t i = get_global_id(0);
    out[i] = coeffs_table[i % 4];
}
```

clang accepts it (`xcrun clang -x cl -cl-std=CL1.2 -fsyntax-only`).

OpenCL input on main at 2c01bef1 binds both the constant and the kernel's buffer at binding 0:

```
@group(0) @binding(0) var<uniform> coeffs_table: array<f32, 4> = {1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f};

fn first(
    @group(0) @binding(0) var<storage, read_write> out: array<f32>
) {
    var i: u32 = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    out[i] = coeffs_table[(i % 4)];
}
```

Metal output refuses the program with an untyped error:

```
ValueError: Conflicting Metal resource binding for 'out': buffer(0) overlaps 'coeffs_table' buffer(0)
```

HIP input had the same fault and #3621 fixed it by numbering `__constant__` bindings first and starting the kernel's buffers after them; `OpenCLToCrossGLConverter` derives from the HIP converter but still binds `out` at 0. A `__constant float *coeffs = coeffs_table;` local in the kernel is also imported as `@group(0) @binding(1) var<uniform> coeffs: ptr<f32> = coeffs_table;`, a resource declaration inside the function.

Expected: OpenCL input numbers program-scope `__constant` bindings before the kernel's buffer and uniform parameters, as CUDA and HIP input do, and Metal output compiles the kernel.

Found while reviewing #3650.

WGSL output writes every brace initializer as `array(...)`, including a structure's.

Reproducer, on `main` at e78faffb:

    shader Probe {
        struct Pair { float a; float b; };
        buffer float values[];
        compute {
            layout(local_size_x = 1) in;
            void main() {
                uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
                float x = values[i];
                Pair p = {x, 2.0};
                values[i] = p.a + p.b;
            }
        }
    }

WGSL output: `var p: Pair = array(x, 2.0);`. naga 30.0.1 rejects it with ``the type of `p` is expected to be `Pair`, but got `array<f32, 2>` ``. GLSL output writes `Pair p = Pair(x, 2.0);` and Metal output `Pair p = {x, 2.0};`, and both compile. A member that is itself an array fails the same way: for `struct Frame { mat3 m[2]; float w; };`, `Frame f = {mat3[2](1.0, x), 2.0};` becomes `var f: Frame = array(array<mat3x3<f32>, 2>(1.0, x), 2.0);`.

Expected: `var p: Pair = Pair(x, 2.0);`, with each value converted to its member's type, as `Pair(x, 2.0)` already is. A brace initializer WGSL output cannot write should be refused with a typed diagnostic.

Found while reviewing #3648.

